ere I feel  like I'm reading something about myself  that rings true and for it is a person  who does that for me so the first thing  is we are not known to ourselves the  second is that the unconscious is both  terrifying and exhilarating  we have depths we have such depths but  there is no theory up to the task of  accounting for the depths that we have  our interior life is so much more  complex than any systematic form of  explanation is capable of providing and  that means that our forms of knowledge  whether we come from scientific  philosophical or literary disciplines  have to have a certain kind of humility  we have to understand the project of  knowledge we'll never really accomplish  or have the measure of what our psychic  lives are and can do and for it says  that for us the third thing I want to  say is that we are infinitely complex  human beings and not just that if I  believe or know that I am infinitely  complex that means that I must also  grant that knowledge or extend that  knowledge to others and that seems to be  a really important thing we might think  about in terms of our social and  political life I spent breakfast this  morning talking as many of you probably  do there the last couple of weeks  talking about Shai Torres and you Kipp  voters and it's really hard to  understand how people behave or act the  ways that we they do and I think one of  the things for it says for us is that if  we are complex then we have to  understand that other people are complex  too they act in mystifying bewildering  ways and I also think that Freud then  allows us to be forgiving and probing  about the ways other people think and  behave the last thing I want to say  about Freud is is that in a way he's  less interested he's not interested in  the phallus he's not interested in the  content of the dream he's interested in  the fact that we dream at all and  psychoanalysis tells it tells us that  we're creative that we're capable of  fantasy but we are replete we have  plenitude and there is something really  creative there is a kind of resource in  the unconscious and in fact it's a  really unruly sort of resource and this  is the very last thing I want to say is  it's the unconscious that makes us  unruly sorts of human beings we are  uncover noble subjects the unconscious  is attached to all sorts of things it's  attached lots of words beginning on the  unconscious is uncertain it is unthought  it is ungraspable it is unknowable it is  uncontainable  it is unexpect  and it comes unbidden that means it's  dangerous the unconscious could kill us  but at the same time it makes us it is  the radical revolutionary principle in  us so in a way I kind of ended up making  a really passionate defense for Freud's  unconscious but I think we all need to  start thinking about that possibility of  the unconscious
 

